#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125401} :activity/id #uuid "91d90bfa-8f19-5546-aba3-f391b0f30b02" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049539 :topic/id #uuid "fcfd540b-2f04-5491-bc34-5138611f81d9" :topic/math-academy-id 1963} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125401 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125783 :tutorial/content "The **image** (or **range**) of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is the set of all vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ that the transformation maps to. That is to say\n\n$$\nT(u)=b\n$$\n\nThe diagram below gives a schematic representation of the image of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}: V \\rightarrow V$ for a vector space $V.$\n\n![](images/b8/b859250756eb3870f9c1d4d7e6e78f044adf2b0c4cd94a87d01011879d668afc.png)\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{0}$ is always in the image of a linear transformation since $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{0})=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the linear transformation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that, for example, the vector $\\mathbf b = [6,9]^T$ is in the image of the transformation $\\mathbf{S}$ since there is another vector $\\mathbf{u} = [1,1]^T$ such that $\\mathbf{S}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{b}{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "70bccafd-705b-5026-bdf8-ef7731dfcc77" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3530 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f42af34b-8f66-5aee-9bd5-7a3de03f5cdf" :step/math-academy-id 20015 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125448}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125402 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124519 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124328 :question/id #uuid "e5f16c73-7422-57db-9f63-d1e593b8c3be" :question/math-academy-id "e-5442"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a08e0a4-22c3-5139-848d-6f1027a61056" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395885}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Is in the Image of a Linear Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "ddfd46cc-2853-59ef-ace0-92fabfa7af70" :step/math-academy-id 20016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125497}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124520 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124329 :question/id #uuid "5b6f9536-7ac0-57d6-b5cd-d9ce4f4e2620" :question/math-academy-id "e-5443"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e1baaf3c-f907-59dc-b9f9-a476a2889e73" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364138}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rank of a Linear Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "eb409d13-a534-5413-97fd-7e71ad7fbfad" :step/math-academy-id 20017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125448 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124547 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124356 :question/id #uuid "372bf9eb-6665-5593-8aab-0fa31f5483d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5619"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4230d6ad-86c4-565e-8c5f-ab08179fc26f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351274}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Component of a Vector in the Image of a Linear Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "905da979-387e-5ded-9c78-3ede6dac59ca" :step/math-academy-id 20424 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125490}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125490 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125819 :tutorial/content "In general, the image of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to the column space of its standard matrix $T.$ That is,\n\n$$\nT\n$$\n\nSo to find out if a vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is in the image of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we just need to check if the matrix equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent." :tutorial/id #uuid "f52415b1-727f-53a7-8502-857fdcb3b835" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4276 :tutorial/title "Determining If a Vector Lies in the Image"} :step/id #uuid "cb766bed-08ee-564c-a45c-c20f57e59be3" :step/math-academy-id 25971 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125402}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125821 :tutorial/content "In general, the rank of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is equal to the dimension of its image:\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T}))\n$$\n\nIf the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ has the standard matrix $T,$ then the dimension of the image is equal to the rank of $T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T})) = \\text{rank}(T)\n$$\n\nSo, in general, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\text{rank}(T).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "89b10851-3eed-5f36-a0ab-c9d0610e9b75" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4382 :tutorial/title "The Rank of a Linear Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "9f45ab6b-4ad9-5e42-8a3f-69ef72e1a03c" :step/math-academy-id 26848 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125403}}] :activity/title "The Image and Rank of a Linear Transformation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124748}]}
